{
  "title": "Il libro {pp:è stato} {pp:pubblicato} e si {ip:vende} bene",
  "translation": "The book has been published and it sells well",
  "topics": "The passive with essere, venire and andare, si as passive and as impersonal",
  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      {
        "h": "Three auxiliaries for the passive"
      },
      "Each one adds its own shade of meaning.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Auxiliary",
            "Meaning",
            "Tenses",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "essere",
              "neutral, or a resulting state",
              "all",
              "Il libro {pp:è stato} {pp:pubblicato}."
            ],
            [
              "venire",
              "the action as it takes place",
              "simple tenses only",
              "Il libro {ip:viene} pubblicato ogni anno."
            ],
            [
              "andare",
              "necessity: it must be done",
              "simple tenses",
              "Questo libro {ip:va} letto."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "*La porta {ip:è chiusa}* can describe a state, whereas *La porta {ip:viene} chiusa alle otto* can only be an action.",
      {
        "h": "Why use the passive?"
      },
      "It moves the focus onto what the action affects, and it lets you leave out who did it. That is why you find it so often in news and official language: *{pp:Sono stati} arrestati due uomini.*",
      {
        "h": "Two different kinds of si"
      },
      "Look at what follows the verb. If there is no direct object, *si* is impersonal and means *people in general*. If there is one, *si* is passive and the verb agrees with it.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Kind",
            "Example",
            "Verb"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Impersonal, no object",
              "In Italia si {ip:cena} tardi.",
              "always singular"
            ],
            [
              "Passive, singular object",
              "Si {ip:vend**e**} la casa.",
              "singular"
            ],
            [
              "Passive, plural object",
              "Si {ip:vend**ono**} appartamenti.",
              "plural"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "Si in more complex sentences"
      },
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Situation",
            "Rule",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "With a reflexive verb",
              "si becomes ci si",
              "Ci si {ip:alza} presto."
            ],
            [
              "With essere + adjective",
              "the adjective is plural",
              "Quando si {ip:è} giovan**i**…"
            ],
            [
              "Past, verb with avere",
              "participle in -o",
              "Si {pp:è mangiat**o**} bene."
            ],
            [
              "Past, verb with essere",
              "participle in -i",
              "Si {pp:è arrivat**i**} tardi."
            ],
            [
              "Past, passive",
              "participle agrees",
              "Si {pp:è vendut**a**} la casa."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "In the past, the auxiliary is always *essere*, even for verbs that normally take *avere*."
    ]
  },
  "use": {
    "exercises": [
